  "summary": "The Oakland City Council voted 7-1 to approve a new two-year, $2.25 million contract with Flock Safety, expanding its ALPR network by 40 PTZ cameras on top of 291 existing ones, despite privacy advocates' objections over federal data access. The council adopted amendments including a ban on sharing data with federal immigration agencies, a two-key approval system for new data-sharing, quarterly independent audits, and a requirement to issue an RFP within 18 months.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "The 7-1 vote approves a new two-year, $2.25 million contract with Flock Safety, an Atlanta-based technology firm that holds major contracts in over 40 Bay Area cities.",
      "paragraph_idx": 2
    },
    {
      "quote": "I don't know how we get up and have several press conferences talking about how we are supportive of a sanctuary city status, but then use a vendor that has been shown to have a direct relationship with Border Control",
      "paragraph_idx": 9
    },
    {
      "quote": "Earlier this year, the San Francisco Standard reported that federal agencies had accessed data from Oakland's cameras. Last month, the city of Richmond deactivated its own camera network after discovering that federal officials could search their database.",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "A 'two-key' approval system requiring both the city's chief privacy officer and the OPD information technology director to authorize any new data-sharing relationships.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "A mandate for quarterly independent compliance audits overseen by the city auditor.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    }
  ],
